ind the  candidates that was going to clean that  side the people and that is something  that I was just virtually impossible  because the Constitution here states  that the ruling coalition party in  parliament has to nominate a candidate  and the president really doesn't have a  say in how that candidate is selected  it's up to the ruling majority parties  in Parliament so constitutionally he  didn't have much power he had to  nominate whoever was given to him by  that group and that on Wednesday that  group which is the Albin ah coalition  who can Parliament that is made up of  foods that are backed by Iran had  nominated the governor of Basra and the  president did not announce that  candidate as the next prime minister and  we believe that to be that he was going  to do that either today or maybe some  point tomorrow that he was just waiting  to find out how people would react to  that nomination but it was very very  clear early on as soon as we heard the  name of that Governor that is has laid  on you the governor that saw that the  people were adamant that they will not  accept that decision you know boxer has  had protests way before we've seen any  of that in Baghdad Bachar is one of the  courts country and provinces in Iraq  even though it has 70% of the country's  oil reserves and major oil fields are  from that province but it really major  the main infrastructure doesn't exist in  that College this is one of the reasons  people have been demonstrating for well  over a year there and they're adamant  that this governor is not part of what  the people on the streets want to be in  government so his decision really the  president's decision violence are not to  go ahead with nominating with announcing  the nomination for the prime minister  was in as a trying an effort to really  approve the people the public that say  they don't want a prime minister who  listen to their needs and demands and  they want to be able to elect their  prime  but unfortunately the country's  Constitution is based on a parliamentary  system and not a presidential one which  is what the demonstrator can want it and  that's what they've been fighting for in  October and the world were 480 people  have lost their lives trying to change  the system the constitution of the  country the Constitution was written in  2005 after many many consultations were  held with various legal groups and the  people of Iraq voted there was a  referendum on this Constitution so they  were a part of the process initially but  now since then they say that the country  has become so corrupt and open to  outside influences namely that of Iran  in the United States that they can no  longer tolerate the system it's not good  enough for them they want to be able to  have the kind of standard of life that  they're entitled to Iraqis or catch  second largest producer of oil over four  and a half million barrels of oil a  producer in Iraq and yet when you are in  the country we don't see that money  anywhere it's not in the infrastructure  or anywhere in this in the country this  is what is the main point of contention  core Iraqis they say they should be able  to have the standard of life that they  want even the wealth that is available  in their country indeed we'll talk about  sort of the technocrats and how a new  candidate could be certainly put into  position as the prime minister but if we  talk about and you touched upon it about  outside influence in internal Iraqi  affairs part of the the gripe you might  say that the public have is about  external influence is about the  influence of Iran and this particular  candidate Adani has been nominated by  you might say the Iranian supported bloc  of parliament what is that relationship  on the ground in society at the moment  about the relationship between Iraqis  and Iranian between Iraqi politicians  that have the support of Iranian styled  either clerics or politicians from  across the border well I think it's a 